"""Introductory child-specific humanitarian planning with fictional scenarios."""
def build(c, m):
    return c(70, 'Child Protection in Emergencies and Displacement', 'Child Protection\nin Emergencies\nand Displacement',
        'Analyse a fictional emergency plan for child-specific risks, separation prevention, coordinated support and accountable information without undertaking field deployment or independent tracing.', [
        m('Child-specific preparedness and coordinated response', 'Identify child-protection responsibilities within a fictional multisector emergency plan.', [
            'Emergencies can change the risks children face and disrupt the relationships and services that support them. A general relief plan may provide essential supplies while overlooking how children reach them, who supervises activities or how a concern is addressed. The CPMS summary provides child-specific standards across protection and other sectors. Use it to identify questions for a fictional plan rather than assume that this short course replaces operational humanitarian training. The focus is child-protection coordination and boundaries, complementing the academy’s wider humanitarian and disaster-response curriculum. [S42]',
            'Preparedness includes understanding existing local capacities, responsibilities and access barriers before an activity begins. Identify who has relevant child-protection competence, how different services coordinate and which arrangements need confirmation. The same coordination structure will not suit every crisis or locality. The CPMS explicitly require contextualisation; a standard’s presence in a handbook does not demonstrate local implementation. In the invented exercise, distinguish confirmed roles from assumptions, identify a responsible review point and avoid promising that every service is available or that the academy has approved a local response system. [S42]',
            'Organisations also need to consider the safeguarding effects of their own operations. Registration, distribution, transport, communications and volunteer management may affect children even when the main activity is relief delivery. KCS guidance supports organisational responsibility for foreseeable risks and suitable response arrangements. A fictional review can identify an inaccessible queue, an unconfirmed supervisor or a risky publicity plan without collecting real child identities. The learner should connect each issue to a suitable owner and an unresolved implementation condition rather than design an unsupervised activity with actual children. [S41]',
            'Roles and competence remain essential under pressure. Completing an introductory course does not authorise emergency deployment, rescue, independent case management or clinical care. A learner can recognise that a plan needs a qualified child-protection role and appropriate local coordination. If a fictional condition exceeds the learner’s scope, record the boundary and the responsible qualified arrangement that would need to exist. Do not invent emergency contacts, legal powers or universal procedures. The purpose is to practise a defensible reading of a plan, including what should remain conditional or paused. [S42, S41]'],
            'An invented flood-response plan lists supplies and volunteers but no child-protection role, accessible distribution arrangement or confirmed route for concerns. A learner is described as ready for independent deployment.',
            'Write a child-protection coordination note identifying three missing arrangements, the relevant responsible roles and the learner’s boundary. Keep all information fictional.',
            'Identify child-specific access, safeguarding and coordination gaps alongside relief provision. Keep deployment and specialist responsibilities with appropriately authorised competent teams.', ['S42', 'S41'], [
                ('A general relief plan may omit child-specific risks.', 'A fictional distribution plan lists supplies but ignores supervision and child access. What should a review add?', 'Child-specific access, safeguarding and qualified coordination questions.', ['A claim that supplies alone establish complete protection.', 'An assumption that children’s needs are identical in every crisis.', 'Independent deployment authority for every learner.'], 'Essential relief provision does not remove child-protection planning responsibilities.'),
                ('Humanitarian standards require contextualisation.', 'An invented team copies a coordination diagram and declares it universally suitable. What is missing?', 'Assessment of the actual setting, existing capacities and appropriate responsibilities.', ['A larger copy of the same diagram.', 'An assertion that local systems are irrelevant.', 'A claim that the course has accredited the response.'], 'Standards are comparators that need suitable local implementation.'),
                ('Operational assumptions must remain distinguishable from confirmations.', 'A fictional plan lists a qualified protection service without confirmation. What should its status be?', 'Unconfirmed, with a responsible verification role and conditional planning.', ['Guaranteed available at every time and location.', 'Already delivered.', 'Replaced automatically by the course learner.'], 'A planning assumption is not evidence of actual capacity or acceptance.'),
                ('An organisation’s own relief operations can create risks.', 'A fictional registration activity publicly displays children’s identities. What should the organisation consider?', 'The privacy and safeguarding implications of its operational design.', ['Only how quickly the list can be photographed.', 'Whether public display automatically improves coordination safely.', 'Whether good intentions remove the need for safeguards.'], 'Relief activities need attention to the risks they may create themselves.'),
                ('Introductory learning does not authorise emergency deployment.', 'A fictional coordinator treats this course completion as independent field-deployment competence. What is appropriate?', 'Retain the learner’s introductory boundary and require the relevant authorised competence and supervision.', ['Proceed with independent rescue and casework.', 'Assume the badge grants statutory powers.', 'Invite children to participate in an unapproved field exercise.'], 'A course is not an operational appointment or proof of specialist competence.')]),
        m('Separation prevention and safe continuity', 'Review a fictional plan for avoiding unnecessary separation and preserving qualified continuity of care.', [
            'Family separation is a distinct protection concern during displacement. The CPMS summary prioritises preventing separation and providing appropriate care and support for unaccompanied or separated children. This does not mean that any adult claiming a relationship should receive a child, or that a learner can independently conduct tracing or reunification. The existing CPS permanency course provides foundational decision domains; this addition examines the child-specific planning gaps that a crisis can create. Keep identity, relationship and care decisions within suitable authorised arrangements for the actual context. [S42]',
            'Ordinary relief practices may unintentionally separate children from caregivers. Queues, transport, registration or activity arrangements should be reviewed for how children and caregivers stay appropriately connected and obtain qualified help if separation occurs. A fictional plan can identify the issue, the responsible role and the need for a suitable local procedure without prescribing a universal tracing method. Avoid publishing a child’s personal details, photographs or location as a classroom solution. A well-intended public appeal may create additional risks if identity, access and sharing safeguards have not been considered. [S42, S41]',
            'Interim care and ongoing support require qualified decisions, appropriate oversight and continuity. A directory entry or informal promise does not demonstrate that a safe arrangement is available. Record what has been confirmed, what remains uncertain and who is responsible for the next review. The CPMS describe alternative care in relation to rights, needs, wishes and best interests, with family-based and stable arrangements prioritised where appropriate. The course does not turn those principles into an individual placement decision, a local legal rule or authority to transport or place a child. [S42]',
            'Information continuity must protect the child as well as support coordination. A referral or handover should use only the information needed for an authorised purpose through appropriate arrangements. Do not equate a sent message with accepted responsibility. In the fictional scenario, distinguish the concern, the qualified receiving role, response status and next check. A learner may identify that a plan is incomplete without filling the gap with real child data or an invented finding. This task is introductory analysis of continuity, not a simulation that authorises actual tracing, reunification or emergency care. [S42, S41]'],
            'An invented evacuation plan uses separate transport queues for children and caregivers. A volunteer proposes posting identifiable photographs online and handing an unaccompanied child to any adult who claims familiarity.',
            'Review the plan for separation risks, information exposure, unverified relationship claims and missing qualified responsibility. Propose conditional corrections without a tracing protocol or real child details.',
            'Prevent avoidable separation through appropriate planning, keep identifying information protected and refer identity/care decisions to suitable authorised teams. Familiarity claims and sent messages are not sufficient evidence of a safe handover.', ['S42', 'S41'], [
                ('Separation prevention belongs in ordinary relief planning.', 'A fictional transport arrangement separates children from caregivers by queue design. What should be reviewed?', 'How the arrangement can avoid unnecessary separation and connect concerns to qualified responsibility.', ['Whether separate queues automatically improve child protection.', 'Only the vehicle count.', 'Whether a learner can approve all transfers independently.'], 'Operational choices can create protection risks that need appropriate planning.'),
                ('A relationship claim is not sufficient proof for a handover.', 'An invented adult claims familiarity with an unaccompanied child. What boundary should a learner recognise?', 'Identity and care decisions need suitable authorised verification and child-protection arrangements.', ['Any familiarity claim establishes a safe relationship.', 'The first claimant should automatically receive the child.', 'A classroom exercise can certify the adult’s authority.'], 'A claim alone does not establish an appropriate care or handover decision.'),
                ('Public identification can create additional risks.', 'A fictional volunteer proposes posting a separated child’s photo and location online. What is the central concern?', 'Potential exposure and misuse of identifying information outside suitable authorised safeguards.', ['That every public post is automatically safe and necessary.', 'Only the image resolution.', 'That the learner should collect more real child images for practice.'], 'Good intentions do not remove privacy and protection risks.'),
                ('Interim care decisions require appropriate authority and oversight.', 'A fictional plan treats an informal offer of accommodation as a verified care arrangement. What is missing?', 'Suitable qualified decision-making, confirmation and oversight for the actual setting.', ['A more confident description of the same unverified offer.', 'A declaration that all available accommodation is safe.', 'An assumption that a course enrolment grants placement authority.'], 'Availability and suitability cannot be inferred from an informal promise.'),
                ('Continuity needs confirmation of responsibility.', 'A fictional handover email receives no response. What should the record state?', 'Handover requested; acceptance and next responsibility remain unconfirmed.', ['All care responsibilities transferred and completed.', 'The child’s needs were necessarily met.', 'The case is ready for automatic closure.'], 'Coordination records must distinguish a request from an accepted arrangement.')]),
        m('Caregiver support cross-sector access and learning', 'Develop a bounded fictional review of child-protection access and evidence in a displacement setting.', [
            'Children’s protection needs connect with education, health, food, shelter and other services. A plan should examine how these sectors interact rather than assume that a single activity addresses every concern. The CPMS cross-sector guidance supports appropriate coordination and attention to access, capacity and existing systems. In an invented review, identify one practical barrier and the responsible sector or qualified role that would need to address it. Keep the action conditional on actual resources and authority. Do not present a classroom plan as an operational service map for a real crisis. [S42]',
            'Caregiver support may be one useful part of a wider response. The humanitarian parenting review found promising but heterogeneous evidence, with most studies conducted in protracted or post-crisis settings rather than the earliest acute emergency phase. This limits claims about what will work immediately after a sudden crisis. The 2026 trial on the Thailand-Myanmar border adds recent evidence from a particular displacement setting, but it does not validate every local programme or establish benefits for every child outcome. Consider competent delivery, contextual fit and access rather than copy intervention sessions or promise a universal effect. [R52, R54]',
            'Participation and accountability remain relevant under disruption. Children and caregivers need appropriate information, suitable ways to raise concerns and a response from responsible roles. An inaccessible channel can hide unmet need rather than demonstrate that no concerns exist. The child-participation review supports attention to relationships and context, while organisational safeguarding guidance keeps implementation responsibility visible. For coursework, use invented aggregate information and an alternative fictional task if preferred. Do not seek testimony from displaced children or recruit families to demonstrate the relevance of an emergency topic. [R51, S41]',
            'A review should distinguish delivery, access and outcomes. Counting sessions, materials or referrals does not establish that all children were reached safely or that protection risks decreased. Describe what the invented figures show, what remains unknown and what qualified follow-up would be needed. A reasonable next step may be a revised access arrangement, a confirmed responsibility or an honest statement that evidence is insufficient. The learner’s proposal does not confer emergency, clinical or statutory competence, and a perfect offline exercise cannot replace verified assessment results, human school-exam review or authorised certificate signatures. [S42, R52]'],
            'An invented displacement programme counts six caregiver sessions and declares all child-protection risks resolved. It offers one inaccessible feedback channel and assumes a study from a protracted crisis proves immediate effects after any disaster.',
            'Write a brief learning review separating delivery, access and protection outcomes. Include the setting limits of the evidence, a qualified owner and one feasible conditional improvement.',
            'The session count is an output. Review who could access support and feedback, keep acute-crisis transfer uncertain and identify a responsible next step without claiming universal effects or independent field competence.', ['S42', 'R52', 'R54', 'R51', 'S41'], [
                ('Child protection requires appropriate cross-sector coordination.', 'A fictional shelter plan ignores children’s access to education and qualified support. What should a review consider?', 'Relevant service connections, access barriers and responsible roles across sectors.', ['A claim that shelter provision alone resolves every concern.', 'A rule that other sectors have no protection implications.', 'Independent clinical tasks for any volunteer.'], 'Children’s needs and protection risks can span several services.'),
                ('Evidence from protracted settings may not transfer to an acute emergency.', 'An invented proposal applies the humanitarian parenting review as a guaranteed immediate effect after a sudden disaster. What needs correction?', 'The transfer claim, given the review’s setting differences, heterogeneity and limited acute-crisis evidence.', ['Only the colour of the proposal cover.', 'The need for contextual planning.', 'The distinction between an evidence review and a field appointment.'], 'Setting and delivery conditions constrain what can be inferred from the evidence.'),
                ('An inaccessible channel can conceal unmet need.', 'An invented programme receives no feedback through a channel many families cannot use. What is justified?', 'The count does not establish absence of concerns; access and response arrangements need review.', ['Every family is satisfied.', 'All child-protection risks have ended.', 'There is no reason to review the channel.'], 'Non-response has multiple possible explanations, including exclusion.'),
                ('Session delivery does not establish complete protection coverage.', 'A fictional report counts six caregiver sessions and declares all children protected. What should be distinguished?', 'Sessions delivered, access to support and evidence about the intended child-protection outcomes.', ['The session count and all outcomes as identical measures.', 'Attendance and statutory care decisions as the same event.', 'An output figure as automatic proof of safety.'], 'Delivery, reach and outcomes require separate evidence.'),
                ('A learning review must preserve competence and recognition boundaries.', 'A fictional emergency portfolio requests a school certificate after an offline quiz and no verified provider result. What is appropriate?', 'Keep provider verification, human school-exam review and authorised signatures unresolved until actually completed.', ['Issue the certificate because the portfolio looks professional.', 'Treat the offline score as deployment authority.', 'Invent the missing Head of Learning signature.'], 'An offline exercise cannot establish assessment verification, professional authority or certificate authorisation.')])
    ])
